        ":question/problem": "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{3}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{3}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{3}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$"
